<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Abkhazia</h1><p class="st-meta">1992 CE – present · capital Sukhumi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Partly recognised state on the Black Sea coast, seen by most countries as part of Georgia; de facto independent since the 1992-1993 war and backed by Russia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Abkhazia was an autonomous republic within the Georgian Soviet Socialist Republic when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> began to break up. Abkhaz leaders under Vladislav Ardzinba declared effective independence from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/georgia">Georgia</a> on 23 July 1992, and war began in August 1992. Abkhaz forces took Sukhumi on 27 September 1993 and soon held nearly all of the territory. Four UN member states recognise it (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, Venezuela, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>); the rest regard it as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>About 5,000 people were killed in the war and up to 250,000 ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> were expelled; the OSCE recognised the ethnic cleansing of Georgians in 1992-1993. The population fell from 525,000 in 1989 to 216,000 in 2012. A ceasefire was agreed in 1994. Sergei Bagapsh won the 2004 presidential election after a first result was cancelled. The Russian ruble is the currency, the economy is integrated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and about half of the budget is financed by Russian aid. Tourism is a key industry, and the country exports wine and fruit.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> recognised Abkhazia on 26 August 2008 after the Russo-Georgian War, and on 28 August 2008 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>'s parliament declared it Russian-occupied. Raul Khajimba became president in 2014 and resigned on 12 January 2020 after protests; Aslan Bzhania won the following election. Protests in November 2024 over an investment agreement with Russia led Bzhania to resign, and Badra Gunba was elected in February 2025 with 56% of the vote.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/abkhazia.jpg" alt="Abkhazia" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Drawn by Achim1999 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_Abkhazia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1992 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sukhumi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vladislav Ardzinba, Sergei Bagapsh, Raul Khajimba, Aslan Bzhania, Badra Gunba</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity, Armenian Apostolic Christianity, Sunni Islam, the Abkhaz native religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Abkhaz, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Abkhazia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2009&amp;focus=abkhazia" aria-label="Abkhazia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5669 646 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Abkhazia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5989 902L6091 947"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6091 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5989 902h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5989 902h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=2009&amp;focus=abkhazia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
